# Classes and Modules

## Classes

class Point
  attr_accessor :x,:y   # creates getter and setter methods for x,y, attr_reader: if read only
  def initialize(x,y)   # constructor, instance method
    @x, @y  = x, y      # creating instance variables
  end
end

-- you can define operator modules like +,-,*,[]= or iterator like:
def each
  yield @x
  yield @y
end

-- to create a mutable class quickly, create it with Struct class that generates classes with accessors for passed fields: 
Struct.new("Point", :x, :y) # creates Struct::Point
Point = Struct.new(:x, :y)  # creates new Point class, you can still use class Point to extend methods 

-- class methods
def self.sum(*points) # can be invoked trough the class itself like Point.sum(...)
    ...
-- class constants -- upper case, defined after initialize, invoked like Point::CONST
-- class variables -- @@var, accessible to class definitions and all instance methods, but any subclass can alter the value for the whole inheritance tree 
-- class instance variables -- @var defined in class body, can't be used from class instance nethods, just class methods, works better with inheritance
-- method visibility: protected, private keywords in class body -- methods after them are protected/private, or can be named specifically like
protected :method_name, :m2 (then it must come after method definition in class body)
-- private can be invoked from other class methods and subclasses, protected also explicitely on the instance of the class

-- subclassing:
class Point3D << Point
-- in subclass we can override parent methods by using the same name, whe can call super() to invoke the parent method to reuse + extend it in subclass

## Modules

-- named group of methods, constants and class variables (like class) but cannot be instantiated or subclassed
-- used as namespaces -- grouping methods when hierarchy and OOP is not necessary, modules can also be nested for namespacing reasons
-- used as mixins -- modules instance methods can be mixed into other classes using include(ModuleName) from within the reciever class body 
-- load / require -- getting modules from different paths


